INTENT ENGINEERING: A FORMAL THEORY OF HUMAN–AI COORDINATION ACROSS VALUE, INTENT, AND ACTIONS .

**Author: Ouadi Maakoul**

**AI Collaborators: ChatGPT, Claude, Gemini, and Future AI Systems**

**Date: juin 2026**

---

## Abstract

Software engineering has historically been the science of constructing static, executable artifacts. The emergence of large language models and agentic AI systems inaugurates a fundamentally different paradigm: one in which the primary design object is not code but *intent*, and in which software is a transient, dynamically generated expression of human values in context. This dissertation establishes **Intent Engineering** as a new scientific discipline — not a software methodology — whose object of study is the full causal chain from human values to world-altering actions, mediated by layers of AI interpretation, planning, and execution.

We make five principal theoretical contributions. First, we establish a **formal five-layer ontology** — Values → Intentions → Plans → Structures → Actions — grounding intent in the philosophy of practical action (Bratman 1987), collective intentionality (Searle 1990, Tuomela 2013), and BDI agent theory (Rao & Georgeff 1991), and demonstrating that *values*, not intents, are the true invariant primitive of any long-horizon human–AI system. Second, we formulate the **Principle of Intent Entropy (PIE)** as a rigorous law, drawing on semantic information theory to show that the semantic content of an intent can only be preserved or degraded — never amplified — as it traverses representational, linguistic, and computational layers. We derive an **Intent Channel Capacity** bound analogous to the Shannon capacity theorem. Third, we introduce **Intent Topology**, a novel formalism establishing a metric space over value-intent pairs, proving compactness conditions necessary for the existence of stable intent representations. Fourth, we define the **Intent Operating System (IOS)** — a computational architecture of five primitives (Intent, Capability, Negotiation, Verification, Governance) and five kernel operations — that treats intent as a first-class runtime object. Fifth, we develop **Intent Economics**, grounding the coordination of billions of conflicting intents in mechanism design and social choice theory, and proving an **Intent Aggregation Impossibility Theorem** that characterises necessary trade-offs in any civilisational-scale intent market.

The grand challenge is articulated as: *How can civilisations computationally understand, negotiate, and realise their collective intentions while preserving value alignment under the thermodynamic constraint of the Principle of Intent Entropy?* This question is shown to subsume AI alignment, democratic deliberation, economic coordination, and cognitive science into a single formal programme. We conclude with a multi-decade experimental research agenda spanning formal methods, multi-agent systems, political philosophy, and cognitive science.

