## Chapter 3: Positioning Against Existing Work

### 3.1 Intent-Oriented Programming

Chen et al. (2025) introduce *intent-oriented programming* as a paradigm in which the developer specifies *what* rather than *how*, with AI systems responsible for generating correct implementations. This work is the closest existing predecessor to Intent Engineering. However, it remains scoped to the software engineering domain and treats intent as an input artifact to a code generation pipeline, rather than as a formal object with its own theory.

The key limitation is what Microsoft Research (2026) calls the *intent gap*: the distance between informal natural language requirements and precise program behavior. The intent gap has always existed in software engineering; agentic AI systems amplify it because they generate entire features autonomously without the continuous human oversight that characterized earlier tool-assisted development. Lahiri et al. (2026) argue that *intent formalization* — the translation of informal intent into checkable formal specifications — is the central grand challenge for reliable AI-assisted coding. This dissertation subsumes and generalizes this challenge: intent formalization is one instance of the broader problem of intent preservation across representational layers, which the Principle of Intent Entropy (Chapter 5) addresses at the level of a physical law.

### 3.2 AI Alignment

The AI alignment literature (Russell 2019, Leike et al. 2023, Bai et al. 2022) is concerned with ensuring that AI systems pursue human values rather than proxy objectives. Value alignment is thus a proper sub-problem of Intent Engineering: it concerns the fidelity of the AI system's objectives to human values, which is one step in the full chain from Values to Actions.

Recent work on normative value alignment (Edelman et al., 2025) introduces *thick model* approaches that represent values as structured tuples with justificatory networks — converging with the formal value representation proposed in Chapter 4. The key difference is scope: alignment research is primarily concerned with training-time value learning, while Intent Engineering addresses the full runtime chain from human intent specification to world-altering action. The Principle of Intent Entropy applies at every layer of this chain, including but not limited to the training-time alignment layer.

### 3.3 Human-Computer Interaction and Agentic AI

The HCI literature on agentic AI (Schömbs et al. 2025, Holmström & Carroll 2025) identifies critical coordination challenges: the delegation gap (humans use AI tools extensively but delegate little fully), opacity of multi-agent systems, and the need for meaningful human oversight of distributed AI action. Zhao and Liang (2026) propose a triadic framework for human–AI collaboration across system, collaboration, and metacognitive layers.

Intent Engineering provides a formal foundation for addressing these challenges. The IOS (Chapter 9) specifies the computational infrastructure required for meaningful delegation, and the Negotiation primitive addresses the conflict resolution required for true human–AI collaboration.

### 3.4 Software Engineering 3.0

Harmon and Reed (2026) characterize *Software Engineering 3.0* as a paradigm in which AI systems take primary responsibility for implementation, with humans responsible for specification and oversight. This characterization aligns with the intent-driven paradigm this dissertation formalizes. However, SE 3.0 remains an engineering paradigm; Intent Engineering proposes a *scientific* discipline concerned with the formal theory underlying that paradigm.

