## Chapter 11: Shared Autonomy and Delegation

### 11.1 The Delegation Spectrum

Human–AI collaboration exists on a spectrum from full human control to full AI autonomy. At one extreme, the human specifies every action; at the other, the human specifies only terminal values and the AI determines everything else. Neither extreme is optimal for most real-world tasks.

**Definition 27 (Delegation Level).** The *delegation level* $\delta \in [0,1]$ of an intent $i$ specifies the fraction of the value-to-action translation chain that the human delegates to the AI system. At $\delta = 0$, the human specifies actions directly (traditional programming). At $\delta = 1$, the human specifies only terminal values and the AI determines intents, plans, structures, and actions.

**Proposition 4 (Entropy-Delegation Trade-off).** The total intent semantic entropy accumulated in the translation chain is a monotonically increasing function of the delegation level $\delta$:
$$H_{\text{total}}(i; \delta) = \sum_{k=1}^{k^*(\delta)} \Delta H_{L_k}(i)$$
where $k^*(\delta)$ is the number of layers delegated. Higher delegation means more entropy, which means more potential for intent-to-action misalignment.

**Corollary 5.** Optimal delegation level is not maximal autonomy but the $\delta^*$ that maximizes $F(i, \sigma)$ — intent fidelity — given the costs of human attention spent on specification and oversight. This is the fundamental engineering trade-off of the IOS.

### 11.2 The Shared Autonomy Architecture

The *shared autonomy* architecture (drawing on robotics literature: Dragan & Srinivasa 2013) extends to the intent domain. In shared autonomy, the human and the AI system jointly control the trajectory of a task, with the AI dynamically adjusting its level of initiative based on its estimate of human intent fidelity.

**Definition 28 (Shared Intent Control).** A *shared intent control law* is a function $u: \mathcal{I} \times [0,1] \to \mathcal{P}$ that maps a partially specified intent and a fidelity estimate to an action plan, blending human-specified constraints with AI-generated plans:
$$p = u(i, F) = F \cdot p_{\text{AI}}(i) + (1-F) \cdot p_{\text{human}}(i)$$
When the AI has high confidence in its intent interpretation ($F$ close to 1), it acts with more autonomy. When fidelity is low, it defaults to human-specified actions.

